<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanghyang Tapak inscription</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1030">1030</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Sanghyang Tapak inscriptions, Jayabupati inscription</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sanghyang Tapak inscriptions are four stones found near Cibadak in Sukabumi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a>, dated to 1030 CE. They record that the Sundanese king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-jayabupati">Sri Jayabupati</a> declared a stretch of the river sacred, forbade fishing there and called down curses on anyone who broke the ban. They are among the earliest dated records of the Sunda Kingdom and show royal authority over sacred land.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanghyang-tapak-inscription.jpg" alt="Sanghyang Tapak inscription" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanghyang_Tapak_inscription.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The text runs to 40 lines, which needed four stones to hold them. All four were found on the bank of the Cicatih River at Cibadak, three near Kampung Bantar Muncang and one near Kampung Pangcalikan. They are written in the ancient Javanese Kawi script and are now kept in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-museum-of-indonesia">National Museum of Indonesia</a> under the numbers D 73, D 96, D 97 and D 98. The inscription is also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-jayabupati">Jayabupati</a> or Cicatih inscription.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The first stone opens with a date in the Saka year 952, in the month of Kartika, on the twelfth day of the bright half of the month, and names the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> with a long string of titles beginning Maharaja Sri Jayabupati Jayamanahen Wisnumurti Samarawijaya. The king made his marks on the eastern part of Sanghyang Tapak and forbade anyone to break his law: fishing was banned in that part of the river, in the sacred area near its source, whose borders were marked by two great trees.</p><p>The fourth stone, of 20 lines, holds the king's oath. It calls on all the supernatural powers and the hyang, the deities of heaven and earth, to protect and support the king's command, and threatens anyone who breaks the law with death at their hands, by having the brain sucked out, the blood drunk, the body disembowelled or the chest split in two. It ends with the words I wruhhanta kamung hyang kabeh, an address to all the spirits.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The reading of the first three stones goes back to the scholar Pleyte. The date is thought to correspond to 11 October 1030. The Pustaka Nusantara gives <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-jayabupati">Jayabupati</a> a reign of twelve years, from 952 to 964 Saka, or 1030 to 1042, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carita-parahyangan">Carita Parahyangan</a> calls him Prabu Detya Maharaja, the 20th king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarusbawa">Tarusbawa</a>. Unexpectedly, the script, language and style of the inscriptions are East Javanese, and the king's titles resemble those used at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmawangsa">Dharmawangsa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sanghyang Tapak inscription” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…securely dated record naming a king of Sunda is the Kebon Kopi II inscription near Bogor, generally read as dated to 932, and the <b>Sanghyang Tapak inscriptions</b> of 1030 near Sukabumi record a ruler called Sri Jayabupati who declared a stretch of river sacred and forbade fishing there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sunda-kingdom">Sunda Kingdom · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
